  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA strengthening ridge of high pressure is building over the \nSouthern Plains today with a trough sitting just off the east \ncoast of Northern New England. This puts us in the \"Ring of \nFire\" around the ridge helping to keep our daily precipitation \nchances elevated. Given that we are starting to get later in the\nyear and our days getting shorter with a lower sun angle, we \nwill not build up a lot of instability most afternoons despite \ngood shear in place, with 0-6 km shear between 30 and 50 knots \nover the next week. Clouds will also help in lowering the amount\nof solar heating we get so most days, we only see a few hundred\njoules of CAPE develop. Subtle shortwaves today and tomorrow \nwill lead to widely scattered rain showers and isolated \nthunderstorms. Surface based CAPE will be limited through \nTuesday and mid level lapse rates are between 5C/km and 6C/km \nwill greatly limit the severe thunderstorm potential today and \ntomorrow. Tuesday and Wednesday have more sun and a more potent \nshortwave in the models currently, though these days will be \nfocused on in key message 2. Beyond Wednesday, troughing may \nbuild back in, but daily showers may still pop up in the \nafternoons under the cooler air aloft. \n\nWith the increase in moisture and likely some rainfall daily, \nvalley fog will be prevalent most mornings if there is any \nclearing of the clouds in the overnight hours. Locally dense fog\nwill form in the typical spots, the valleys of the Chemung, \nSusquehanna, and Delaware Rivers. The Wyoming and Mohawk Valleys\nmay even get some dense fog if clearing overnight could last \nmore than a few hours Monday night and Tuesday night. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nTuesday and Wendesday this week look to have late season severe\npotential. The ridge builds a little bit more into the \nNortheast, with warmer, more humid conditions pushing in helping\nto build better Surface Based CAPE. Looking at Ensemble \nprobabilities of greater than 1000 J/kg of surface CAPE and bulk\nshear greater than 30 knots, both Tuesday and Wednesday \nafternoons have a greater than 50% chance of meeting those \nrequirements. Lowering CAPE to 500 J/kg, probabilities get over \n80% Tuesday and 70% Wednesday. Deterministic models vary more \nbut there is good consistency in Tuesday having between 30 and \n40 knots of shear with CAPE closer to 1000 in the model means. \nWednesday has better shear, with models consistent with shear \ncloser to 50 knots. Wednesday also has the stronger shortwave \npassage. \n\nPutting it all together, Tuesday's potential is a little lower \nwith less synoptic support for triggers, but still could be \nsome isolated to scattered thunderstorms in the afternoon. With \nhodographs mostly strait and NW flow, hail and damaging wind \ngusts would be the main concern. Wednesday, hodographs are more\ncurved with the passage of the shortwave and have longer tails.\nIts tough to figure out what the storm evolution may be, as \nthere is plenty of synoptic lift. This could cause it to me \ncloudy and limit surface CAPE and keep storms more elevated with\nhail and localized downburst being a concern. If it does end up\nbeing warmer and sunny with surface based CAPE, all hazards are\npossible. Storms may upscale quick into an MCS as well with the\nstrength of the shortwave so flash flooding will likely become \na concern especially later in the day and overnight Wednesday. A\nlow level jet and plenty of heat and moisture in the ridge \nfeeding into the shortwave may help sustain storms and upstream \nredevelopment well into the night.",
